      "problem": "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $X?$\n\n- $X∨(X∨Y)$\n- $(Y∧X)∨X$\n- $Y∨(X∧Y)$",
      "worked_solution": "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nX∨(X∧Y)≡X.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $X.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $X.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Y∧X)∨X & ≡X∨(Y∧X) \\\\  & ≡X∨(X∧Y) \\\\  & ≡X.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∨(X∧Y) & ≡Y∨(Y∧X) \\\\  & ≡Y≢X.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
